Thanks for the "non-psycho" answer. I'm well aware of what sudo is and
what it's for. But creating directories owned by root is beyond what
linux allows it to do and so as a result installing nethack *has* to be
done *by* root. This same reason is why a *lot* of packages have to be
installed by root (or the install process doing it as the case may be).
The limitations of sudo are OT here, buy for those who *are* interested,
try looking in /usr/doc 


Besides, the very things that a compile/install requires (see below from
man sudo) are cancelled by running sudo. 


First paragraph of security notes in sudo(1):

sudo tries to be safe when executing external commands.  Variables that
control how dynamic loading and binding is done can be used to
subvert the program that sudo runs.  To combat this the LD_*,
RLD_*, SHLIB_PATH (HP-UX only), and LIBPATH (AIX only)
environment variables are removed from the environment passed on
to all commands executed. sudo will also remove the IFS, CDPATH,
ENV, BASH_ENV, KRB_CONF, KRB- CONFDIR, KRBTKFILE, KRB5_CONFIG,
LOCALDOMAIN, RES_OPTIONS, HOSTALIASES, NLSPATH, PATH_LOCALE,
TERMINFO, TERMINFO_DIRS and TERMPATH variables as they too can
pose a threat. 


Specifically LD_* variables are used by link in a compile to find the
libraries referenced by the include files is the source. 

